//! # Wire data and serialization
//!
//! `#[wire]` marks records, enums, and machines whose schema can cross a
//! service or remote-actor boundary. Fields and variants receive stable numeric
//! identities. Assign explicit `@N` tags for a published schema, and reserve a
//! deleted tag instead of reusing it.
//!
//! ```hew
//! #[serial(case = "camelCase")]
//! #[wire(version = 1, min_version = 1)]
//! type User {
//!     id: u64 @1;
//!     display_name: string @2;
//!     email: Option<string> @3 optional;
//!     reserved @4;
//! }
//!
//! fn main() {
//!     let user = User {
//!         id: 7,
//!         display_name: "Ada",
//!         email: .None,
//!     };
//!     println(user.display_name);
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! Required `Option<T>` fields encode `None` as a present null. An
//! `optional Option<T>` field omits its key when it is `None`. Changing between
//! required and optional is incompatible. Unknown fields are discarded when
//! decoding, so decoding and re-encoding does not preserve them.
//!
//! [CBOR](std.encoding.cbor.html) is the binary wire and inter-node format.
//! [JSON](std.encoding.json.html) and [YAML](std.encoding.yaml.html) are text
//! interchange formats. JSON uses field names;
//! `#[serial(case = "camelCase")]` sets a type-wide convention and
//! `#[serial(key = "name")]` overrides one field. JSON encodes `bytes` as
//! base64 text, while CBOR uses byte strings.
//!
//! Run `hew wire check --against path/to/published/schema` in CI. It rejects
//! reused or changed tags, incompatible field presence, and other breaking
//! changes. Generic wire declarations are schema families: each concrete
//! argument must itself be admissible when encoded or sent remotely.
